/**
 * The creation bytecode of ENS's own `PermissionedRegistry`, verbatim.
 *
 * This is the contract a `.eth` name needs before it can issue subnames at all.
 * A name registered through the ENSv2 beta has none — `getSubregistry` answers the
 * zero address — and `/register` cannot supply one, because the registry has to
 * exist before the name that will own it does.
 *
 * ## Why the bytecode is vendored instead of an address
 *
 * There is no factory for this one. Unlike the resolver, which is a UUPS proxy
 * behind `VerifiableFactory` and therefore cheap to point at a shared
 * implementation, a registry is deployed whole: one `PermissionedRegistry` per
 * name, each holding its own token table. So the browser has to carry the
 * bytecode.
 *
 * Compiled from `contracts/ens-src/PermissionedRegistry`, which is ENS's source
 * unmodified. `npm run fork:subregistry` deploys from this constant on a fork and compares
 * the runtime code it produces against a registry already live on this
 * deployment, so a stale or truncated copy fails a check rather than a user's
 * transaction.
 *
 * Kept in its own module so that `connect.ts` can `await import()` it. It is
 * 31,236 bytes of hex; every page that imports
 * `connect.ts` would otherwise pay for it, including the ones that never deploy
 * a registry.
 *
 * Constructor: `(ILabelStore labelStore, address rootAccount, uint256 roleBitmap)`.
 */
export const PERMISSIONED_REGISTRY_BYTECODE =
  "" as const;
